Additional grants and notices:

- daemon/ is Apache-2.0 (daemon/LICENSE).
- kernel_module/ is dual-licensed MIT OR GPL-2.0-or-later. Both halves of that
  dual licence are load-bearing, and neither is optional:

    * the GPL-2.0-or-later half is what lets the module bind kernel symbols
      exported with EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL (the MEI client bus and the ccp driver's
      platform-access API). A module the kernel does not consider free is
      refused those symbols outright;
    * the MIT half is what keeps the source permissively reusable, so it can be
      distributed under this repository's Apache-2.0 umbrella and reused
      outside a kernel tree.

  Every declaration in the module agrees on this: each source file carries
  "SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR GPL-2.0-or-later", and every MODULE_LICENSE
  in the module states "Dual MIT/GPL" — the ident Linux defines for exactly
  that pair (include/linux/module.h). Plain "GPL" there would silently drop the
  MIT half and, with it, the permissive reuse this project depends on.

  Note that combining the module with the kernel at build/load time is what
  brings GPL-2.0 obligations to the resulting binary; the MIT option governs
  the source on its own.
- ramdisk/ and scripts/ are MIT OR GPL-2.0-or-later. The full text of both
  licences ships in ramdisk/LICENSE-MIT and ramdisk/LICENSE-GPL. Nothing in
  either directory links against the kernel — they are ordinary user-space
  programs and shell scripts — so unlike the kernel module, the MIT option
  survives into the built binaries.

