The daemon (Apache-2.0) and the kernel module (MIT OR GPL-2.0-or-later) are
separate works that communicate only through a text interface: the tokens the
module publishes on /proc/sysentinel_metrics. That token vocabulary necessarily
appears on both sides — it is the protocol — but no implementation is shared
across the boundary, and the daemon links nothing from the module.

Performance-counter and CPU-topology code (daemon/src/pmu.rs,
daemon/src/coretype.rs) is Apache-2.0 and incorporates no kernel source:

- The perf_event_open constants and the perf_event_attr layout are the Linux
  syscall ABI, taken from include/uapi/linux/perf_event.h. That header is
  licensed "GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note", and the Linux-syscall-note
  exception states that user programs using kernel services through normal
  system calls are not derived works.
- Hybrid/heterogeneous CPU support is derived clean-room from processor
  vendors' own public architecture manuals, not from kernel source:
  Intel(R) 64 and IA-32 Architectures Software Developer's Manual, order
  no. 325462, Table 21-63 "Leaf 1AH Native Model ID Enumeration"; and Arm(R)
  Architecture Reference Manual, ARM DDI 0487, section D13.2.98 "MIDR_EL1".
  Both are cited in the source and were verified against hardware.
- The privilege-ladder behaviour of perf_event_paranoid is documented as
  observable behaviour in our own words; the implementation probes for it
  rather than encoding it.

